<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maurice, Prince of Orange</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1567–<a href="/years/1625">1625</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Maurice of Nassau · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maurice of Nassau, Prince of Orange from 1618, was stadtholder of Holland and Zeeland from 1585 and later of most other provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>, he reformed the army with his cousin William Louis, introducing regular drill, smaller units and volley fire, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-stevin">Simon Stevin</a> he recaptured many towns from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the 1590s. He defeated a Spanish army at Nieuwpoort in 1600 and in 1619 had Oldenbarnevelt executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange.jpg" alt="Maurice, Prince of Orange" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: School of Michiel Jansz. van Mierevelt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:School_of_Michiel_Jansz._van_Mierevelt_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Maurice of Nassau</b>, stadtholder from 1585, and his cousin William Louis reformed the army with regular pay, smaller units, constant drill and volley fire, and with the engineer Simon Stevin they recaptured towns such as Breda in 1590 and won the Battle of Nieuwpoort in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1590s <b>Maurice of Nassau</b> and his cousin William Louis, commanding the forces of the Dutch Republic in its war against Spain, studied the military manuals of classical antiquity and drilled their musketeers to fire by ranks: each rank fired and then withdrew to reload while the…</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li><li><blockquote>He had clashed with Stadtholder <b>Maurice of Nassau</b> amid the conflict between Remonstrants and Counter-Remonstrants.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-13">13 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch sailors named Mauritius after Prince <b>Maurice of Nassau</b> in 1598, and in 1638 the Dutch East India Company (VOC) founded Fort Frederik Hendrik at Grand Port to forestall French and English rivals.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-mauritius">Dutch Mauritius</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Maurice of Nassau</b> besieged and took the city in 1594, and it joined the Dutch Republic as a province.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-groningen">Lordship of Groningen</a></li></ul></section>
